.TH git-new-version "1" "November 2026" "SMI Toolset 0.156.0" "SMI Git Command"
.SH NAME
git-new-version \- start a new version of the project in a git flow feature branch
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git new-version
\fINEW_VERSION\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBgit new-version\fR command changes the version of the project in the feature branch
\fBnew-version-\fINEW_VERSION\fR and finishes it, each step printed as \fBExecuting: ...\fR before it runs:
.IP 1. 4
\fBgit flow feature start new-version-\fINEW_VERSION\fR.
.IP 2. 4
\fBgit flow feature list\fR.
.IP 3. 4
\fBsmi-new-version\fR \fINEW_VERSION\fR: the version markers of the project, by its \fB.new-version\fR file.
.IP 4. 4
\fBgit add .\fR
.IP 5. 4
\fBgit save "New version started"\fR: commit and push.
.IP 6. 4
\fBgit flow feature finish new-version-\fINEW_VERSION\fR: merge into the develop branch.
.PP
It is run in the project root, with git flow initialized there (\fBgit flow init\fR, for example by the
git-flow-next package). It stops on the first failing step and stays on the feature branch then, for
example when \fINEW_VERSION\fR is no semantic version or there is no \fB.new-version\fR file.
.PP
Git runs it as a subcommand because the executable \fBgit-new-version\fR is in the \fBPATH\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.B git new-version 0.158.0
.SH AUTHOR
.PP
Command written by Imre Tabur.
